<title>TURKMENISTAN: Ukraine to ship Turkmens 200,000 T grain for gas.</title>
<headline>Ukraine to ship Turkmens 200,000 T grain for gas.</headline>
<dateline>ASHGABAT 1997-06-24</dateline>
<text>
<p>Ukraine will ship 200,000 tonnes of grain to Turkmenistan worth a total of $60 million by the end of July, to help pay off its debts for Turkmen natural gas, the Ukrainian Ambassador to Turkmenistan said on Tuesday.</p>
<p>Vadim Chuprun told Reuters the grain would cover debts for deliveries of Turkmen natural gas to Ukraine in 1996 on contracts guaranteed by the Ukrainian government.</p>
<p>&quot;Thus, officially Kiev will have no debts to Turkmenistan, except for the rescheduled debts for Turkmen gas deliveries made in 1993,&quot; he said.</p>
<p>The rescheduled debt totals $704 million, which the Ukrainian government started servicing in January this year, Chuprun said.</p>
<p>Last year Turkmenistan faced severe bread shortages due to a paltry grain harvest, and was forced to spend $800 million on massive grain imports. Debts among former Soviet republics for Turkmen natural gas, meanwhile, have reached $1.8 billion.</p>
<p>--Marat Gurt, Almaty Newsroom, +7 3272 509410</p>
</text>
